Chapter: SecretsNameSort
  cites Secrets chapter SecretEntry
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 `secret-sort-name` had no caller anywhere in the tree, so both secrets
 tests passed while it sorted vault names by ADDRESS. It compiled, and
 `<` on Text was a pointer compare, so the order was whatever order the
 entries happened to be allocated in. CDX2089 is what surfaced it; this
 file is what keeps it surfaced.

 The pair is chosen so the two candidate orders DISAGREE. CCE numbers its
 characters by frequency rather than alphabetically, so `d` sorts before
 `b` in encoding order and after it in collation order. A sort written
 with `text-compare` therefore passes a test built from any pair whose
 letters happen to agree, and fails this one.

 That is the whole point of the file: it does not merely assert that the
 function answers, it asserts WHICH of the two orders it answers in.
 Reverting the body to `text-compare a.se-name b.se-name < 0` moves both
 lines below.

Section: Fixtures

  banana : SecretEntry
  banana = secret-new 1 SecretPassword "banana" "u" "" 0

  date : SecretEntry
  date = secret-new 2 SecretPassword "date" "u" "" 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("banana-before-date=" & show (secret-sort-name banana date))
    print-line-uni ("date-before-banana=" & show (secret-sort-name date banana))
    print-line-uni ("encoding-says-d-first=" & show (text-compare "date" "banana" < 0))
  end

